# glassgen relaxation knobs for runtest.sh glass ICs (distrib 2). Sourced by
# runtest.sh; the glass and IC file names carry a short tag of these knobs
# (e.g. Gc2oif1), so changing one relaxes a fresh glass instead of reusing one.
#
# SPH_KERNEL is the kernel the glass is relaxed with. The run codes keep their
# own kernel in codes/<code>/build_config*.sh; a code whose build differs from
# SPH_KERNEL prints a warning at build resolution (the glass is force-free only
# under its own kernel).
SPH_KERNEL=kerneloptimized          # wendlandc2 | wendlandc4 | kerneloptimized | sinc

# Pair-force operator, gasoline's CORRPARTITION family (default = gasoline's
# default GDFORCE+ISPH build):
GLASS_SIGMA=0                  # density partition exponent (dFacQ); only 0 = plain SPH density
# GLASS_SIGMA_F and GLASS_NEIGHBOURS may be overridden from the environment (e.g. a testing
# script relaxing glasses for a CORRPARTITION TSPH operator); unset = the values here.
GLASS_SIGMA_F=${GLASS_SIGMA_F:-1}                # force/gradient volume exponent (dFacQf): 1 = GDSPH, 0 = TSPH
GLASS_GRADIENT=onesided-isph   # onesided-isph | onesided-sph | average-isph | average-sph
GLASS_NEIGHBOURS=${GLASS_NEIGHBOURS:-constant}   # constant (kNN h) | variable (h from m/rho)
# ISPH fallback threshold (gasoline boundterm0 = 0.05) and gasoline's grad-h owner
# factor 3/tr(C T) (CORRPARTITION_GRADH_EXACT, one-sided gradients). 0.05 with 1 relaxes against
# gasoline's exact t=0 operator.
GLASS_ISPH_BOUNDTERM=${GLASS_ISPH_BOUNDTERM:-2.0}
GLASS_GRADH=${GLASS_GRADH:-0}                    # 1 = apply the grad-h factor

# Density target and stiffness:
GLASS_KERNEL_MATCH=1           # 1 = compare rho with the kernel-averaged target (--kernel-match)
GLASS_RHOPOW=2.0               # pseudo-pressure exponent, pP = (rho/rho0)^rhopow

# Relaxation schedule (glassgen.cli; all may be overridden from the environment). A value other than
# the default here tags the glass name (n<max>, i<icr0>, l<icrloop0>, r<rate>, ii<n>, st<stop>, pi<n>, np,
# ft<TOP>).
GLASS_MAXITER=${GLASS_MAXITER:-4800}             # -n: iteration cap per relax stage
GLASS_ICR0=${GLASS_ICR0:-1.0}                    # --icr0: initial move, as a fraction of the box
GLASS_ICRLOOP0=${GLASS_ICRLOOP0:-0.5}            # --icrloop0: re-anneal restart level / icr0
GLASS_ICR0RATE=${GLASS_ICR0RATE:-1.5}            # --icr0rate: step-controller rate
# How a relax ends. The finish acts in the final (polish) phase of the full-resolution stage:
# GLASS_ICR0_STOP stops once the largest step per iteration, as a fraction of h (median over 10
# iterations), is below it; 0 = off. GLASS_POLISH_ITER > 0 stops after that many polish iterations.
# Whichever fires first ends the run; with both 0 it runs to GLASS_MAXITER. The step-size stop says the
# step controller has become cautious, not that the glass has settled: after many cascade levels (large
# N) and with many neighbours (polish is entered at a step of 0.025 nSmooth^-1/3 h = 3.4e-3 at 400) it
# fires within ~30 iterations and leaves percent-level density noise (soliton 128^3, n400: 5.6 % in
# the core; GLASS_ICR0_STOP=0 GLASS_POLISH_ITER=300 gives 0.16 %).
GLASS_ICR0_STOP=${GLASS_ICR0_STOP:-3e-3}
GLASS_POLISH_ITER=${GLASS_POLISH_ITER:-0}
# Healing iterations on each intermediate level of the cascade (after every split but the last).
GLASS_INTER_ITER=${GLASS_INTER_ITER:-10}
# Multi-resolution cascade: 1 = relax a coarse glass, then split every particle in two and heal, level
# by level up to the full number (fast; glassgen's default). 0 = one full-resolution relax
# (--no-progressive).
GLASS_PROGRESSIVE=${GLASS_PROGRESSIVE:-1}
# FFT coarse-grid correction of the long-wavelength density modes (glassgen/coarse.py). 0 = off.
# TOP > 0 (2 is the tested value) = on (--coarse-fft TOP): the re-anneal ladder starts at TOP x h and
# the correction is applied at every rung end and at polish entry. Needs GLASS_PROGRESSIVE=0 (the
# cascade has no such correction) and a periodic box; not with an open (envelope) boundary or
# GLASS_FORCEMATCH (glassgen stops).
GLASS_COARSE_FFT=${GLASS_COARSE_FFT:-0}
# With GLASS_COARSE_FFT on, N > 0 = transport mode (--coarse-fft-every N): the correction is also applied every N
# iterations, with R from the median h and strain-capped steps, for a smooth profile far from its pre-IC. The
# rung-end-only default never converges there (soliton n400: the first rung waits for the residual to go, the
# |u|/h cap applies 3-8 % per correction and the mesh cannot see the core). Soliton 64^3, N 10: core scatter of
# ln rho 4.5e-4 against the cascade's 1.9e-3, in 2-4x its time. Not for sharp compact objects (areablob: the
# blob empties). 0 = off.
GLASS_COARSE_FFT_EVERY=${GLASS_COARSE_FFT_EVERY:-0}

# Target shape. 0 = raw: the profile as the setup gives it. 1 = curvature step (glassgen/shaping.py): the
# narrowest monotone step between the two plateaus of a 1D .param contact (kh only) built to hold the
# pressure error GLASS_SHAPE_ERRP = max|ln pPs|, which then also replaces GLASS_PBUMP_WARN in the IC build's
# check. A setup or a feature it cannot serve is refused. Tags the glass "cv<err_P>". Kernel match above is a
# separate switch and still acts on either target, so a truly raw target also needs GLASS_KERNEL_MATCH=0.
# The err_P fit is calibrated to facQf 0, sigma 0, variable neighbours (64), boundterm 0.05, grad-h, onesided-isph,
# the O64 kernel and a point read with no kernel-match; other operators deliver a different err_P. The IC build
# reports the value delivered.
GLASS_TARGET_SHAPE=${GLASS_TARGET_SHAPE:-0}
GLASS_SHAPE_ERRP=${GLASS_SHAPE_ERRP:-0.02}

# Force-matched relax (glassgen --force-table): instead of
# fitting the density with the pseudo-pressure, the glass works on the real pressure A rho^Gamma
# plus the table's g = grad P_t/rho_t, so it ends with the target's pressure acceleration in the
# operator's discrete form (density close to, not exactly, rho_t). For a hydrostatic target
# (polytrope) g is its gravity and the glass is hydrostatic; for a collapse IC (evrard) g only
# holds the target. 1 needs a setup that writes a force table with its pre-IC (polytrope, evrard:
# forcetable.npz; accdisk: datafiles/<prefile>.force.npz on (R, |z|); runtest.sh stops for a test
# without one). Tags the glass "fm". Not with GLASS_START_P=pseudo.
GLASS_FORCEMATCH=${GLASS_FORCEMATCH:-0}

# Pressure the IC starts with on the glass (applied after IC_createsetup.py builds the IC,
# setupfiles/IC_glasspressure.py). The glass is force-free under its pseudo-pressure
# pP = (rho/target)^rhopow, which carries a bump b ~ A/w^2 at density features of width w
# (A ~ 1 h^2 for contrast 2-10):
#   uniform = the setup's pressure as it sets it (default). At a sharp
#             feature the run starts with a flow v/c_s ~ b/gamma and settles into the bump.
#   pseudo  = the setup's pressure x pP/median(pP): starts in the glass's force balance,
#             quiet (v at the noise level), but the bump stays as a pressure error.
#             Only for setups that keep the glass particles as they are (no crop /
#             replicate / move); IC_glasspressure.py refuses others.
# The IC name (not the glass name) carries "pP" when pseudo; run names are left as they are.
GLASS_START_P=${GLASS_START_P:-uniform}
# Warn when the glass's kernel-averaged pseudo-pressure bump max|ln pPs| exceeds this
# (a relative pressure error; the smoothed noise floor is ~1.5e-3).
GLASS_PBUMP_WARN=${GLASS_PBUMP_WARN:-0.01}

# Diagnostics: 1 = also write ${glass}_denserr.png (final density-error and
# residual-force histograms) and ${glass}_history.png (errors and step over the
# whole cascade, levels marked). The ${glass}_IC.{rho0,rho0h,pP,fres,funi} aux
# arrays are always written.
GLASS_PLOTS=1
